    expectedAnswer:
      'CODING is implementing a specific function/class correctly; SOFTWARE DESIGN is organizing code within a single application (classes, modules, patterns like the repository pattern); ARCHITECTURE is the set of high-level structural decisions about an application (layering, module boundaries, dependency direction) that are expensive to change later; SYSTEM DESIGN is architecture at the scale of MULTIPLE cooperating services/components (which datastore, how services communicate, how the whole system scales/fails) — the defining distinction across this whole spectrum is the COST OF CHANGE: a naming choice is cheap to change tomorrow, a database choice is expensive to change in six months, and a "monolith vs microservices" decision can be enormously expensive to reverse a year later.',
    deepExplanation:
      '```text\nCoding          — "how do I implement this function correctly and efficiently?"\n                  cost of a wrong choice: minutes to fix\n\nDesign          — "how should these classes/modules within ONE application relate?"\n                  (e.g. repository pattern, dependency injection, a specific design pattern)\n                  cost of a wrong choice: hours to days to refactor\n\nArchitecture     — "what are this APPLICATION\'s high-level structural decisions?"\n                  (layered vs hexagonal, module boundaries, dependency direction,\n                   sync vs async internally) — decisions that ripple through the whole\n                   codebase and are genuinely expensive, though not impossible, to reverse\n                  cost of a wrong choice: weeks to months to refactor\n\nSystem Design    — "how do MULTIPLE services/components cooperate as a SYSTEM?"\n                  (which datastore per workload, how services communicate, how the whole\n                   system scales, what happens when one component fails)\n                  cost of a wrong choice: months, sometimes requiring a fundamental\n                  re-architecture across an entire organization\n```\n\nThe PRACTICAL, interview-relevant reason this distinction matters: it tells you WHERE to spend deliberation time. Over-analyzing a coding-level decision (bikeshedding a variable name for twenty minutes) wastes time relative to its low cost of being wrong; under-analyzing a system-design-level decision (picking a database or a service boundary without working through the tradeoffs) is disproportionately costly precisely because it is SO expensive to reverse later. A senior/staff engineer\'s judgment is largely about correctly calibrating HOW MUCH analysis a given decision deserves, proportional to how expensive it would be to get wrong and change later — not simply "always think harder about everything".\n\nA concrete worked example spanning all four levels for the SAME feature ("add product reviews"): CODING — implementing a function that computes a product\'s average star rating correctly, including rounding behavior. DESIGN — deciding a `ReviewRepository` class encapsulates all review persistence logic, injected into a `ReviewService`. ARCHITECTURE — deciding reviews belong in the SAME service/database as products (not a separate "Reviews Service"), because they are read together on every product page and have no independent scaling/team-ownership need yet. SYSTEM DESIGN — deciding, at a MUCH larger scale, whether a dedicated review/rating aggregation pipeline (perhaps event-driven, updating a cached aggregate rating asynchronously) is needed once the product catalog reaches a scale where computing average ratings on every read becomes a genuine bottleneck.',
